   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_MsncjQBmZRqw8s5s",
   "name": "Efficiency of energy transfer",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "837314078"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Efficiency of energy transfer"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **efficiency **of a system is a measure of the amount of **useful **and **wasted energy** in an energy transfer\n- Efficiency is defined as:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**The ratio of the useful power or energy output from a system to its total power or energy input**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This means that:\n\n  - if a system has **high** efficiency, this means most of the energy transferred is **useful**\n  - if a system has **low** efficiency, this means most of the energy transferred is **wasted**\n- The overall efficiency of a typical thermal power station is approximately 30%\n\n  - This means that 70% of the energy transferred from the power station to the National Grid is **wasted energy**\n- In the production of electricity:\n\n  - Energy is used to heat water to produce steam\n  - The steam turns a turbine\n  - The turbine turns a generator\n  - The generator produces electricity\n  \n    - At each stage of this process, energy is dissipated to the surroundings"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Sankey diagram of electricity production"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/09/cie-1-8-6-sankey-diagram-of-power-station-efficiency.png",
   "file": "assets/01a5113d7af6-cie-1-8-6-sankey-diagram-of-power-station-effici.png",
   "alt": "cie-1-8-6-sankey-diagram-of-power-station-efficiency",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Sankey diagram showing the energy transfers involved in generating electricity in a gas-fired power station***"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_k2V2CrW8rjspnMHF",
   "name": "Calculating efficiency",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Calculating efficiency"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Extended Tier Only"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Efficiency is represented as a percentage and can be calculated using two equations"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Efficiency in terms of **energy**:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\text{efficiency} = \\frac{useful \\text{energy} output}{total \\text{energy} input} ( \\times 100 \\% )$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Efficiency in terms of **power**:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\text{efficiency} = \\frac{useful \\text{power} output}{total \\text{power} input} ( \\times 100 \\% )$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "An electric motor has an efficiency of 35%.\n\nIt lifts a 7.2 kg load through a height of 5 m in 3 s.\n\nCalculate the power of the motor.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Step 1: Write down the efficiency equation in terms of power**\n\n$e \\text{fficiency} = \\frac{useful \\text{power} output}{total \\text{power} input} ( \\times 100 \\% )$\n\n**Step 2: Rearrange to make power input the subject**\n\n$\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{input}=\\frac{\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{output}}{\\mathrm{efficiency}\\div100}$  **OR**  $\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{input}=\\frac{\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{output}\\times100}{\\mathrm{efficiency}}$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the power output**\n\n$\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{output}=\\frac{\\DeltaE}{t}$\n\n- $\\DeltaE$ is equal to the change in gravitational potential energy as the load is lifted\n\n$\\DeltaE_{P}=mg\\Deltah$\n\n$\\DeltaE_{P}=7.2\\times9.8\\times5$\n\n$\\DeltaE_{P}=352.8J$\n\n- Therefore:\n\n$\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{output}=\\frac{352.8}{3}$\n\n$\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{output}=117.6W$\n\n**Step 4: Substitute the values into the power input equation**\n\n$\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{input}=\\frac{117.6}{0.35}$  **OR**  $\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{input}=\\frac{117.6\\times100}{35}$\n\n$\\mathrm{power} \\mathrm{input}=336W$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "Efficiency can be given in a ratio (between 0 and 1) or percentage format (between 0 and 100 %)\n\nIf the question asks for efficiency as a ratio, give your answer as a fraction or decimal.\n\nIf the answer is required as a percentage, remember to multiply the ratio by 100 to convert it:\n\n- if the ratio = 0.25, percentage = 0.25 × 100 = 25 %\n\nRemember that efficiency has **no units**"
  },
